## 10. The `switch` Statement

A **multiple-alternative** decision structure. The value of a variable/expression decides which path runs. It can replace an `if-else-if` that tests the same variable against several values.

```csharp
switch (testExpression)
{
    case value_1:
        statement;
        break;
    case value_2:
        statement;
        break;
    default:
        statement;
        break;
}
```

- `testExpression` can be an `int`, `string`, or `bool` value.
- Each `case` holds one or more statements and ends with `break`.
- `default` is **optional**; it runs when no case matches.

## 11. Introduction to List Boxes

A **ListBox** displays a list of items and lets the user select one.

### Common Properties

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `Text` | Text of the currently selected item (`string`) |
| `Items` | The collection of items in the ListBox |
| `Items.Count` | Total number of items |
| `SelectedItem` | The currently selected item (`object`) |
| `SelectedIndex` | Zero-based index of the selected item (`-1` if none) |

```csharp
selectedFruit = fruitListBox.SelectedItem.ToString();
```

### Adding Items

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Add("Monday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Tuesday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Wednesday");
```

### Selected Item & Index

- Index starts at **0**: the first item is `0`, the *n*th item is `n - 1`.
- Reading `SelectedItem` when nothing is selected causes an **exception**.
- Always check first:

```csharp
if (fruitListBox.SelectedIndex != -1)
{
    selectedFruit = fruitListBox.SelectedItem.ToString();
}
```

### `Text` vs `SelectedItem`

| Feature | `Text` | `SelectedItem` |
|---------|--------|----------------|
| Type | `string` | `object` |
| Use case | Quick access to selected text | Full access to the selected item |
| Flexibility | Less flexible | More flexible (can be cast) |
| Nothing selected | Returns `""` | Returns `null` |
| Best for | Simple text-only lists | Complex or data-bound lists (e.g., `Student`, `Book` objects) |

### Removing Items

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Remove("Tuesday");   // remove a specific item
daysListBox.Items.RemoveAt(2);         // remove item at index 2
daysListBox.Items.Clear();             // remove all items
```

> `RemoveAt` throws an exception if the index is `< 0` or `>= Items.Count`.

